{{also|Quietus}}
==English==

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*kʷyeh₁-}}
Short for {{lbor|en|ML.|[[quiētus]] [[est]]|lit=he is quit|notext=1}}. {{etydate|1530s. Earlier attested as Late {{cog|enm|quietus est}}.}}<ref>{{R:Etymonline}}</ref> {{dbt|en|quiet|coy}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/kwaɪˈiːtəs/}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|-|+}}

# A [[stillness]] or [[pause]]; something that [[quiet]]s or [[repress]]es; removal from [[activity]].
#* {{RQ:James Bostonians|page=284|chapter=XXX|passage=Olive's specific terrors and dangers had by this time very much blown over; Basil Ransom had given no sign of life for ages, and Henry Burrage had certainly got his '''quietus''' before they went to Europe.}}
# {{lb|en|figuratively}} {{cap|death}}.
#: {{syn|en|demise|fatality|Thesaurus:death}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Hamlet|act=III|scene=i|page=265|passage=When he himſelfe might his '''''Quietus''''' make / With a bare Bodkin?}}
#* {{quote-book|1=en|year=1887|author=Harriet W. Daly|title=Digging, Squatting, and Pioneering Life in the Northern Territory of South Australia|page=336|passage=After a good deal of firing, and lying in wait - for every time he heard a shot down he'd go, and on coming to the surface, would only expose about two or three inches of his nose to fire at - we managed to give him his '''quietus'''.}}
# Final [[settlement]] (e.g., of a debt).

